 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The threshold magnitude is 0.8 V to 2.0 V, taken with gate tied to drain at 250 µA. On-resistance is specified at a single point, 5 V of gate drive and 100 mA: 5.0 Ω typical, 10 Ω maximum. Typical on-resistance at 25 °C and light load is about 4 Ω with 4.5 V on the gate and about 3.4 Ω with 10 V. At 25 °C and VDS = 10 V, 0.5 A flows with roughly 3.5 V on the gate."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "On-resistance rises with junction temperature: equal to 1 near 30 °C on the normalized scale, it is about 0.65 to 0.7 at -55 °C and about 1.66 to 1.77 at 145 °C. At 4.5 V of gate drive it reaches roughly 6.3 to 8.8 Ω at 150 °C across the 0 to 0.53 A range. The transfer curves for -55, 25 and 150 °C cross near 3 V and 0.25 A: below that point a hotter part conducts more, above it less."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate charge",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "With 40 V on the drain and 0.5 A, the gate climbs to a plateau of about 3.4 V after roughly 380 pC, stays there to about 1000 pC, then rises to 8 V at about 2000 pC. Total gate charge is 6000 pC typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The intrinsic diode conducts from drain to source. It is rated 0.130 A continuous and 0.520 A pulsed, with a typical forward drop of 2.5 V. At 25 °C it passes about 1 mA at 0.7 V, 0.1 A near 1.4 V and 0.77 A near 2.4 V; at 150 °C the same currents need roughly 0.35 V to 0.5 V less."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Dissipation is 225 mW with the ambient at 25 °C and junction-to-ambient resistance is 556 °C/W. The junction and storage range is -55 °C to 150 °C. Leads may be soldered at 260 °C for 10 s."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grades and materials",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Both packing options come in two grades. Part numbers starting with S- are for automotive and similar uses needing a controlled site and change process; they are AEC-Q101 qualified and support PPAP. A lead-free package is available; the material is halogen-free and RoHS compliant."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The top of the package carries the device code PD followed by a one-character month code."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Intended for small power-management circuits: DC-DC converters and load switches in battery-run gear, for example phones, computers and printers."
  }
 ],
